From fc5f37d8aab1e77a3233366fc41ea922a1ca216b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alberto Leiva Popper <ydahhrk@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 12:39:16 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Remove snmp_fold_field() preprocessor conditional

Someone decided to remove the EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL() of snmp_fold_field()
in kernel 7.2, so @iam-TJ proposed this preprocessor branch in pull
request #456.

This commit is my review of it.

The patch works perfectly fine as it is, but I decided to remove the
preprocessor conditional because of the following train of thought:

RHEL kernels haven't yet catched up to the EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL() removal
quirk, so technically, LINUX_VERSION_AT_LEAST(7, 2, 0, 0, 0) should be
LINUX_VERSION_AT_LEAST(7, 2, 0, 9999, 0), to exclude all current RHEL
kernels from the positive (expanded snmp_fold_field()) branch.

That being said, the positive branch happens to also work for old RHEL
kernels, because since the implementation of snmp_fold_field() hasn't
changed in a long time, copying the implementation does not cause any
problems in any supported RHEL.

In fact, while "9999, 0" is technically more correct, "0, 0" is more
future-proof because RHEL will eventually catch up and remove the
export, at which point I'm going to need to crawl back to this and
update "9999, 0" into whatever RHEL version is giving trouble at the
time. So forcing all RHELs to pipe through the positive branch is less
work.

And this logic can be expanded to regular kernels. snmp_fold_field()'s
implementation has remained still since kernel 4.3, and Jool's earliest
supported kernel ATM is 5.15...

If we're forced to copy the snmp_fold_field() implementation into our
own code, might as well do it for all supported kernels.
---
 src/mod/common/stats.c | 4 ----
 1 file changed, 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/mod/common/stats.c b/src/mod/common/stats.c
index b689314..31190a0 100644
--- a/src/mod/common/stats.c
+++ b/src/mod/common/stats.c
@@ -81,13 +81,9 @@ __u64 *jstat_query(struct jool_stats *stats)
 		return NULL;
 
 	for (i = 0; i < JSTAT_COUNT; i++) {
-#if LINUX_VERSION_AT_LEAST(7, 2, 0, 0, 0)
 		int cpu;
 		for_each_possible_cpu(cpu)
 			result[i] += snmp_get_cpu_field(stats->mib, cpu, i);
-#else
-		result[i] = snmp_fold_field(stats->mib, i);
-#endif
 	}
 
 	return result;
-- 
2.56.0

